 "asked_at": "2026-10-17T16:45+08:00",
 "answer": "**For a short trip outside, you probably don’t need a mask if you’re otherwise healthy.** But Singapore is experiencing haze today, **October 17**: the National Environment Agency forecast air quality in the *unhealthy to very unhealthy* range and advises healthy people to avoid prolonged or strenuous outdoor exertion. ([nea.gov.sg](https://www.nea.gov.sg/media/news/advisories/index/haze-situation-update-16-october-2026))\n\nIf you **must spend several hours outdoors**, a well-fitting **N95** can help filter haze particles; a regular surgical mask is not effective against the fine particles. If you’re pregnant, older, or have a heart or lung condition, minimise or avoid outdoor activity rather than relying on a mask alone. ([healthhub.sg](https://www.healthhub.sg/highlights-and-insights/health-safety-advisory/how-to-protect-yourself-against-haze))\n\nCheck the **1-hour PM2.5 reading** for your part of Singapore in NEA’s myENV app before heading out—the conditions relevant to an immediate outing can change during the day. ([nea.gov.sg](https://www.nea.gov.sg/media/news/advisories/index/haze-situation-update-16-october-2026))",
